297:\subsection{Vertex Cover Linear Program}
299:The vertex cover problem can be expressed as an integer linear program where an optimum solution of the integer linear program would correspond to a minimum vertex cover. The variables of the linear program take values in $\{0,1\}$. However, we can relax this constraint on the variables and allow them to instead take on any real value in the interval $[0,1]$. This is called the LP relaxation of the vertex cover problem (also known as the fractional vertex cover problem)~\cite{lovasz2009matching}. It is defined as follows.
303:While finding the minimum vertex cover problem is NP-hard, finding the optimum solution for the fractional version can be done 
306:The dual of the vertex cover linear program is the LP relaxation of the maximum matching problem and is given as follows.
312:As the vertex cover linear program is a relaxation of a minimization problem, the optimal solution to the vertex cover linear program is always less than or equal to the size of the minimum vertex cover. Similarly, the fractional maximum matching size is always greater than or equal to the size of the maximum matching. Since $\mu_f$ and $vc_f$ are duals of each other, their optimal values are the same. Hence we have the following inequality.
318:The fractional versions of these problems have been widely studied~\cite{NT75,lovasz2009matching,pulleyblank1987fractional,balas1981integer,nemhauser1974properties,trotter1973solution,picard1977integer}. 
319:For the vertex cover LP, it is known that there always exists an optimum solution where every variable takes values in $\{0,\frac{1}{2},1\}$~\cite{nemhauser1974properties,trotter1973solution}.
320:For an optimum solution to the vertex cover LP, let $S_1$ and $S_0$ be the set of vertices that get assigned $1$ and $0$ respectively. One may observe that there is a matching across the edges $(S_0,S_1)$ that saturates $S_1$. Suppose not, then by Hall's Theorem, there exists a subset $A \subseteq S_1$ such that $|N(A) \cap S_0| < |A|$. Now we may construct a smaller feasible solution by setting the vertices in $A \cup (N(A) \cap S_0)$ to $\frac{1}{2}$ and keeping the value of the other vertices the same. It is easy to see that this is feasible and has smaller value.
321:%Understanding the fractional versions can sometimes be used to get faster algorithms for certain problems~\cite{LNRRS14}. 
322:% Some useful properties of the fractional vertex cover are given in the following classical theorem.
325:\begin{theorem} [Nemhauser-Trotter Theorem] \label{thm:nem_trotter} \cite{NT75,picard1977integer} 
326:Given a graph $G$, there exists a polynomial time algorithm that computes an optimum solution to the LP of Vertex Cover of $G$ such that
331:\item There exists a matching between $S_0$ and $S_1$ that saturates $S_1$.
332:% \item There exists a minimum vertex cover of $G$ that contains all vertices in $S_1$ and no vertices in $S_0$.
333:% \item The solution that sets every variable to $\frac{1}{2}$ is the unique optimum solution for the fractional vertex cover on the graph induced by $S_{\frac{1}{2}}$.
337:% We will also require the following theorem, which provides a nice characterization as to when there exists a matching between two sets of vertices.
339:% \begin{theorem} [Hall's Theorem] \label{thm:halls} \cite{hall1935representatives}
340:% Let $G$ be a bipartite graph with bipartite sets $X$ and $Y$. For a subset $W$ of vertices in $X$, let $N_Y(W)$ denote the neighborhood of $W$ in $Y$. Then there exists a matching across the cut $(X,Y)$ saturating $X$ if and only if for every subset $W$ of $X$, $|N_Y(W)| \geq |W|$.
