523:\section{{\Konig} Edge Deletion disjoint from Matching}
525:As the goal of the {\Konig} edge deletion problem is to delete edges to make the vertex cover size equal to the maximum matching, a natural question is whether the minimum {\Konig} edge deletion set should be allowed to delete edges of a maximum matching. This is a strong restriction since there are some graphs in which the minimum {\Konig} edge deletion set must pick some edges from a maximum matching of the graph. 
526:See Figure \ref{fig:counterexample} for an example of such a graph.  In the figure, the matching shown is the only maximum matching of the graph and all the minimum {\Konig} edge deletion sets of the graph intersect this matching (shown is a particular minimum {\Konig} edge deletion set).
633:\caption{A graph where the minimum {\Konig} edge deletion set must pick an edge from the maximum matching. The dotted edges denote the {\Konig} edge deletion set. The dotted edges plus the bold edges are the edges in the maximum matching}
637:\defproblem{{\sc {\Konig} Edge Deletion disjoint from Matching}}{Graph $G = (V,E)$, a maximum matching $M$ of $G$ and integer $k$}{Does there exists $F \subseteq E$ such that $|F| \leq k$ and $G[E \setminus F]$ is {\Konig} and $F \cap M = \emptyset$?}
639:A similar variant was studied with respect to edge deletion to stable graphs. A graph is said to be stable if the optimum fractional maximum matching of the graph is equal to the size of its maximum matching. 
641:Note that due to Lemma \ref{lem:konig_alternate}, {\Konig} graphs can be characterized as the class of graphs for which the optimal fractional vertex cover is equal to the minimum vertex cover size, thus {\Konig} edge deletion and stable edge deletion are very similar problems. The problem of deleting $k$ non-matching edges (for a particular maximum matching) such that the resulting graph becomes stable was looked at by Bock et. al. where they prove that this variant of the problem remains NP-hard~\cite{bock2015finding}. 
643:We show that our variant of {\Konig} edge deletion problem remains NP-hard, but fixed-parameter tractable when parameterized by the solution size.
