+
+NOTES = r"""
+Section 2, Definition 2.1 fixes Konig to mean equality between maximum-matching
+size and minimum-vertex-cover size.  Lemma 2.2 supplies the executable witness:
+a vertex cover S and a matching across (S,V-S) saturating S.  Section 3 is the
+decisive construction.  Claim 3.3 maps an independent set I to the private
+edges {V_i,P_i}; its proof writes both S=(V-I) union C and a matching of the
+same size.  Claim 3.4 proves the converse, while Corollary 3.1 proves that no
+smaller deletion set exists.
+
+Step-0 algorithm check.  Theorem 5 gives an FPT algorithm only for the different
+variant in which a maximum matching M is supplied and deletions must avoid M;
+via Almost-2-SAT its stated running time is O*(2.31^k).  This generator does not
+use that easy regime: its certified deletions are private edges belonging to a
+perfect matching of G'.  For the unrestricted Section 3 problem, Theorem 2 is
+W[1]-hard and Theorem 3 preserves that hardness even when G' has a perfect
+matching.  The certificate is not produced by a polynomial algorithm: it is
+known because I is sampled before G and then carried through Claim 3.3.
+
+The base distribution is a balanced planted-3-CNF conflict graph.  A truth
+assignment is sampled first.  Each clause uses three distinct variables and a
+uniform sign pattern having either one or two literals true under the plant;
+this gives every occurrence an unbiased literal sign and certifies the formula.
+The standard clause-triangle/contradictory-occurrence construction then gives
+an independent set containing one true occurrence per clause.  A final uniform
+relabeling removes clause positions and displayed edge order as signals.  The
+panel tests degree outliers, minimum-degree
+greedy, randomized greedy restarts, and a standard exact maximum-clique search
+on the complement using greedy coloring bounds.  Difficulty comes from a larger
+ambient graph and a slightly larger k, not from a long certificate.
+
+The canonical key hashes an isomorphism-invariant collection of degree and
+common-neighbor histograms of the base graph.  It is invariant under arbitrary
+renumbering and under input-edge reordering, but it is not a complete graph
+isomorphism test; that limitation is stated in the README caveats.
+""".strip()
