-## What the problem is and why the result is trustworthy
-
-[Majumdar, Neogi, Raman, and Vaishali, *Tractability of König Edge Deletion Problems*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.04560) defines a graph as König when its maximum-matching size equals its minimum-vertex-cover size. The generated problem gives an exact succinct description of an undirected graph (K) and asks for at most (k) edges whose deletion makes (K) König. An answer contains one residue per block; residue (a_i) names the deleted pendant edge `{X(i,a_i),P(i,a_i)}`.
+## What the problem is
 
-Generation is inverse, not solution by search. The module first samples an assignment over (mathbb F_q), makes every affine-permutation constraint true on it, and chooses the unique cycle's multiplier to be nonidentity. The assignment is therefore the unique independent set selecting one value per block. Section 3's reduction adds a pendant vertex for every source vertex and (2k) independent universal vertices, carrying that independent set to the requested deletion set. `verify` never reads `inst["answer"]`: it checks every modular relation exactly. Those checks certify that the selected source vertices are independent; the paper's explicit cover and saturating matching can then be reconstructed symbolically, so their equal sizes certify the König property.
+[Majumdar, Neogi, Raman, and Vaishali, *Tractability of König Edge Deletion Problems*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.04560) asks for at most (k) edges whose removal makes maximum-matching size equal minimum-vertex-cover size. The generator uses the paper's Section 3 construction verbatim: a source Independent Set instance receives one private vertex per source vertex and (2k) independent universal vertices.
 
-This is licensed-reduction coverage, not a claim that the generated distribution is a generic hard distribution of unrestricted explicit König-deletion graphs. The paper's native graph and deletion edges remain visible, but the search is carried by the affine constraint representation. The succinct encoding is also materially smaller than expanding the graph, which has 240,000,576 vertices at the shipping preset.
+The source graph is specified succinctly by (n) blocks of (q) vertices and affine compatibility constraints. A submitted vector selects one vertex per block and therefore names the (n) private edges to delete. `verify` checks every affine constraint exactly modulo the prime (q). Those checks establish an independent set; Claim 3.3's explicit cover and matching then have the same cardinality, certifying that the deleted graph is König. The planted vector is sampled before the constraints are built, so generation never solves its output.
 
-## Why it is hard on Track B
+## Why this is Track B
 
-The paper's Section 3, Theorem 2 proves König Edge Deletion W[1]-hard by a parameter-preserving reduction from Independent Set for (k<|V(H)|/2), and Theorem 3 keeps that hardness even when the constructed graph has a perfect matching. Worst-case W[1]-hardness is **not** the claim for this special distribution. An efficient algorithm exists here: choose a value for one cycle vertex, propagate the affine permutations around the cycle, and reject it unless it closes. This takes (O(qL+n)) exact operations. At `hard`, the reference implementation succeeds 8/8 as expected and averages 2,662,175 root trials, 47,919,202 modular operations, and 2.735864 seconds on the final shared-host run.
+Theorem 2 proves unrestricted König Edge Deletion W[1]-hard and Theorem 3 retains hardness when the graph has a perfect matching. That worst-case statement is not the hardness claim here. This structured distribution has algorithms: after fixing a spanning tree, finite-domain root enumeration costs (O(q\ell+n)), where \(\ell\) is the sole cycle length. At the shipping preset the measured eight-instance mean was **2.735864 s**, **2,662,175 root trials**, and **47,919,202 exact modular operations**; it solved 8/8 as expected.
 
-The compact route is to compose the (L=9) maps into (x\mapsto Ax+B), solve the one equation (x=Ax+B), and propagate once through the attached trees. The conservative count is 152 exact arithmetic operations. The difficulty is noticing this invariant and executing it without a CAS; it is not a Track-A complexity claim.
+The compact route composes the cycle maps to (x\mapsto Ax+B), solves the single fixed-point equation (x=Ax+B\pmod q), and propagates once through the tree. It is bounded at **152 exact operations**. The benchmark tests whether a no-tool solver can recognize and execute that compression accurately, not whether the instance lacks an algorithm.
 
-Section 4, Theorem 5 identifies an easy regime that must be stated explicitly: when a maximum matching (M) is supplied and deletions must avoid (M), the task reduces to Almost-2-SAT and is FPT; the paper quotes an (O^*(2.31^k)) algorithm. No compatible (M) is supplied here. A perfect matching disjoint from the unique answer can be constructed after the answer is known, while an arbitrary supplied perfect matching generally turns that restricted instance into a NO instance. Small (q), an affine-aware solver, or disclosure of the cycle composition also makes this family easy.
+Section 4, Theorem 5 is the easy regime deliberately avoided: when a compatible maximum matching is supplied and deletions must avoid it, the problem reduces to Almost-2-SAT and is FPT in (O^*(2.31^k)). No matching is supplied here. A perfect matching disjoint from the unique deletion set can be written down only after that deletion set is known.
 
