index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..96f5793d6ae77ddef493dea6e30a9cba6958ee55
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,158 @@
+# arXiv 1811.04560 — verified König Edge Deletion generator
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | CSP/SAT |
+| Certificate form | integer tuple naming pendant deletion edges |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: affine holonomy around the unique constraint cycle |
+| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Reduction | paper-licensed, Section 3, Theorem 2 and Claims 3.3–3.4 |
+
+## What the problem is and why the result is trustworthy
+
+[Majumdar, Neogi, Raman, and Vaishali, *Tractability of König Edge Deletion Problems*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.04560) defines a graph as König when its maximum-matching size equals its minimum-vertex-cover size. The generated problem gives an exact succinct description of an undirected graph (K) and asks for at most (k) edges whose deletion makes (K) König. An answer contains one residue per block; residue (a_i) names the deleted pendant edge `{X(i,a_i),P(i,a_i)}`.
+
+Generation is inverse, not solution by search. The module first samples an assignment over (mathbb F_q), makes every affine-permutation constraint true on it, and chooses the unique cycle's multiplier to be nonidentity. The assignment is therefore the unique independent set selecting one value per block. Section 3's reduction adds a pendant vertex for every source vertex and (2k) independent universal vertices, carrying that independent set to the requested deletion set. `verify` never reads `inst["answer"]`: it checks every modular relation exactly. Those checks certify that the selected source vertices are independent; the paper's explicit cover and saturating matching can then be reconstructed symbolically, so their equal sizes certify the König property.
+
+This is licensed-reduction coverage, not a claim that the generated distribution is a generic hard distribution of unrestricted explicit König-deletion graphs. The paper's native graph and deletion edges remain visible, but the search is carried by the affine constraint representation. The succinct encoding is also materially smaller than expanding the graph, which has 240,000,576 vertices at the shipping preset.
+
+## Why it is hard on Track B
+
+The paper's Section 3, Theorem 2 proves König Edge Deletion W[1]-hard by a parameter-preserving reduction from Independent Set for (k<|V(H)|/2), and Theorem 3 keeps that hardness even when the constructed graph has a perfect matching. Worst-case W[1]-hardness is **not** the claim for this special distribution. An efficient algorithm exists here: choose a value for one cycle vertex, propagate the affine permutations around the cycle, and reject it unless it closes. This takes (O(qL+n)) exact operations. At `hard`, the reference implementation succeeds 8/8 as expected and averages 2,662,175 root trials, 47,919,202 modular operations, and 2.735864 seconds on the final shared-host run.
+
+The compact route is to compose the (L=9) maps into (x\mapsto Ax+B), solve the one equation (x=Ax+B), and propagate once through the attached trees. The conservative count is 152 exact arithmetic operations. The difficulty is noticing this invariant and executing it without a CAS; it is not a Track-A complexity claim.
+
+Section 4, Theorem 5 identifies an easy regime that must be stated explicitly: when a maximum matching (M) is supplied and deletions must avoid (M), the task reduces to Almost-2-SAT and is FPT; the paper quotes an (O^*(2.31^k)) algorithm. No compatible (M) is supplied here. A perfect matching disjoint from the unique answer can be constructed after the answer is known, while an arbitrary supplied perfect matching generally turns that restricted instance into a NO instance. Small (q), an affine-aware solver, or disclosure of the cycle composition also makes this family easy.
+
+## Worked demo (`seed=0`)
+
+This is the complete rendered demo instance:
+
+```text
+KOENIG EDGE DELETION IN A SUCCINCTLY SPECIFIED GRAPH
+
+All graphs below are finite, simple, and undirected.  A matching is a
+set of pairwise endpoint-disjoint edges.  A vertex cover is a set S
+meeting every edge.  A graph is Koenig when its maximum matching and
+minimum vertex cover have the same size.
+
+Let q=7 (a prime) and let there be n=4 value blocks, numbered
+0 through 3.  Arithmetic in the constraint list is modulo q.
+First define a source graph H with vertices X(i,a), where
+0 <= i < 4 and 0 <= a < 7.  Its edges are exactly these:
+  (1) all distinct X(i,a), X(i,b) in the same block i are adjacent;
+  (2) a line 'i j s t' means x_j = s*x_i+t (mod q), and for that
+      line X(i,a) is adjacent to X(j,b) exactly when
+      b != s*a+t (mod q).
+There are no other edges in H.  The constraint-block graph is connected
+and has exactly one cycle.
+
+Constraint lines (i j s t):
+  2 0 5 4
+  3 2 3 4
+  1 3 4 5
+  2 1 4 0
+
+Now define the actual graph K using the paper's construction.  For every
+source vertex X(i,a), add a new vertex P(i,a) and the pendant edge
+{X(i,a),P(i,a)}.  Add 2n new vertices C(0),...,C(2n-1), with no
+edges among them.  Every C(r) is adjacent to every X(i,a) and every
+P(i,a).  Keep all edges of H.  These are all vertices and edges of K.
+Thus K has 64 vertices, though the rules above
+specify it exactly without expanding its repetitive edge set.
+
+Find at most n=4 edges whose deletion makes K a Koenig graph.
+Return the deletion set in its required compressed form: a JSON list
+[a_0,...,a_3] of exactly 4 integers, where entry a_i denotes
+deleting the pendant edge {X(i,a_i),P(i,a_i)}.  Each entry must be in
+the inclusive range 0..6.  Equal values in different blocks are
+allowed; order is fixed by the zero-based block number.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as one JSON list.
+Example shape: <answer>[0, 0, 0, 0]</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The cycle equations can be composed by hand modulo 7, so this smallest supported setting is genuinely hand-solvable. Its answer is `<answer>[6, 3, 6, 3]</answer>`. `verify(inst, [6,3,6,3])` returns `(True, "ok")`; changing the final entry gives `verify(inst, [6,3,6,4]) == (False, "constraint 3->2 fails: got 6, expected 2")`.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | Blocks (n) | Prime (q) | Cycle (L) | Source vertices | Actual (K) vertices | Answer entries |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| demo | 4 | 7 | 3 | 28 | 64 | 4 |
+| easy | 12 | 10,007 | 5 | 120,084 | 240,192 | 12 |
+| medium | 18 | 200,003 | 7 | 3,600,054 | 7,200,144 | 18 |
+| **hard (ships)** | **24** | **5,000,011** | **9** | **120,000,264** | **240,000,576** | **24** |
+
+`easy` was solved by all three bare oracle attempts. `medium` was solved by one of three, so both were rejected by the hardening level test. `hard` held all three attempts.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted witnesses verify |
+| G2 | pass | 5/5 corruptions rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | prose/fence/tag round-trip and JSON-native answer |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 tree-consistent guesses; exact density (1/5,000,011\approx2.0\times10^{-7}) |
+| G5 | pass | demo exact count 1; shipping baseline 2,662,175 trials / 47,919,202 operations / 2.735864 s |
+| G6 | pass | five attacks, each 0/8; reference algorithm 8/8 as expected |
+| G7 | pass | doubled to 48 blocks and cycle length 18; witness still verifies |
+| G8 | pass | 100/100 invariance checks, 20/20 carried witnesses, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9 | pass | 214 chars, 49 lexical tokens, 24 atoms, 152 intended-route operations |
+
+The five failing G6 attacks are equal-degree/minimum-label outlier selection, zero-root greedy tree propagation, one-pass cycle repair, the diagonal ansatz, and 256 uniform tree-consistent restarts. Plants and decoys have identical per-value graph degree inside each block.
+
+## Bare oracle loop
+
+| Preset | Seed | Model | Solved | Why |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| easy | 1583111490 | Gemini 3.8 Flash | yes | verified witness |
+| easy | 947536894 | GPT-5.6 Terra | yes | verified witness |
+| easy | 234512307 | GPT-5.6 Terra | yes | verified witness |
+| medium | 611366817 | GPT-5.6 Terra | yes | verified witness |
+| medium | 1427823153 | Gemini 3.8 Flash | no | modular constraint failure |
+| medium | 597627168 | Gemini 3.8 Flash | no | modular constraint failure |
+| hard | 88264326 | GPT-5.6 Terra | no | modular constraint failure |
+| hard | 65171038 | Gemini 3.8 Flash | no | modular constraint failure |
+| hard | 2086254882 | GPT-5.6 Terra | no | modular constraint failure |
+
+The script-owned verdict is `hardened`, with `hard` as the shipping preset after two escalations.
+
+## G9 diagnostic arms
+
+| Arm | Solved / attempts | Interpretation |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0/3 | shipping evidence |
+| structural | 0/3 | the invariant alone did not produce a verified answer |
+| placebo | 1/3 | one verified answer despite no structural information |
+
+`hinted - placebo = -1/3`. With only three attempts per arm, this is noisy, but it provides no evidence that naming the invariant helped; the placebo success shows why prompt changes cannot be credited to the hint. Two unsolved arm replies exhausted their response while doing arithmetic and emitted no tagged answer, so `parse_answer=None` was correct rather than a parser defect. G9(c) is comfortably within the caps: 214 characters, 24 atomic entries, and 152 intended exact operations.
+
+## How to use it
+
+```python
+from gen_1811_04560 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=123, **DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+print(render(inst))
+ok, reason = verify(inst, inst["answer"])
+assert (ok, reason) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit deterministic JSONL instances with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1811.04560 20
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+- The density prior is deliberately strong: `random_candidate` first enforces a canonical spanning tree, leaving only one uniformly random root among (q). The exact (1/q) success rate is meaningful for that prior, but says nothing about a nonuniform affine-aware strategy—which is precisely the compact solution.
+- This is Track B. An affine solver, CAS, or short custom script solves every instance efficiently; the reference algorithm is disclosed rather than hidden among failed attacks.
+- The graph is succinct. Expanding its vertices and repetitive clique/conflict edges would be infeasible in the no-tool prompt, and the paper analyzes explicit rather than succinct input. This representational choice is why `domain_essentiality` is `licensed_reduction`, not `native`.
+- `canonical_key` is proved invariant under constraint order, block relabelling, independent affine value gauges, edge orientation, and their composition. It does not solve full isomorphism under arbitrary non-affine permutations of millions of value labels; it uses the strongest cheap invariant for the family.
+- No commercial SAT/SMT package or arbitrary graph-isomorphism package was run. The relevant specialized algorithm—finite-domain propagation—is implemented and measured, and the still faster affine composition is the intended route.
+- Wall-clock time varied with shared-host load (roughly one to three seconds in calibration); the deterministic 47,919,202 mean operation count is the stable baseline.
